---
import Product from '../../layouts/Product.astro';
import ProtectedRegistryApiFlow from '../../components/ProtectedRegistryApiFlow.astro';
import { products } from '../../data/products';
const product = products[1];
---
<Product
  product={product}
  heading="Publish selected registry data."
  description="Registry Relay gives other services a controlled interface to look up and read selected data from an existing registry. Define the fields, their meaning, and the rules for access, while keeping record maintenance in the source system."
>
  <section id="product-example" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="relay-example-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">One example: information for a public assistance service</p>
        <h2 id="relay-example-title">Share enrollment details without the rest of the household record.</h2>
        <p>A public assistance service needs a household’s reference, district, and enrollment status to process an application. The registry team agrees to provide those fields while withholding the home address and payment details.</p>
      </div>
      <ProtectedRegistryApiFlow />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="relay-access-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">A reusable interface for your registry</p>
        <h2 id="relay-access-title">Define what services can read and how they find it.</h2>
        <p>Publish agreed information about people, households, businesses, facilities, or other records your organization maintains. Define permitted reads and lookups, with consistent field meanings and access rules for receiving services.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>Describe the data you publish</h3><p>Choose the records and fields to expose, and define their names and meaning for other services. Relay produces the interface and its documentation from that reviewed definition.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Give services specific access</h3><p>Specify which reads and lookups are available and the records and fields each service may receive. A service can ask for fewer permitted fields, but its request cannot widen the access you have granted.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Keep context and a record of the read</h3><p>Record identifiers and source context accompany the selected information so the receiving service can interpret it. Relay records the request and its outcome for audit.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="relay-fit-title">
    <div class="section-inner product-fit">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it fits your organization</p>
        <h2 id="relay-fit-title">Keep corrections in the source register.</h2>
        <p>Your organization owns and maintains the records. Registry Relay provides a read-only interface for other services, so a request for information leaves the source records unchanged.</p>
        <p>Agree which information is useful, who may receive it, and how current the published data needs to be.</p>
      </div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Your registry team agrees the information</dt><dd>Define the published records, field meanings, and access each service needs. Keep responsibility for corrections and updates with the source register.</dd>
        <dt>Your technical team prepares the data</dt><dd>Provide the reviewed source view and any extraction or update process it requires. Configure Relay’s interface and access rules, and connect the receiving service.</dd>
        <dt>Choose the response your service needs</dt><dd>When a service needs a defined fact, such as adult status or a residence region, <a href="/solutions/evidence-gateway/">Evidence Gateway</a> can derive and sign that answer from a configured source.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="developers" class="section" aria-labelledby="relay-developers-title">
    <div class="section-inner product-fit">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">For developers and implementers</p>
        <h2 id="relay-developers-title">Publish selected fields from a sample register.</h2>
        <p>The introductory tutorial uses a synthetic business register in SQLite. Publish its legal name and legal form, try a request for the withheld address, and inspect the audit record.</p>
        <p><a class="inline-cta" href={product.tutorialHref}>Run the SQLite publication tutorial →</a></p>
        <a class="inline-cta" href="https://docs.registrystack.org/products/registry-relay/">Read the product documentation →</a>
      </div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Source preparation</dt><dd>Relay reads reviewed, read-only SQLite views. Spreadsheets and other databases require an organization-owned preparation step.</dd>
        <dt>Published interface</dt><dd>The configuration defines resource names, fields, and access and disclosure rules. Relay generates the API contract and returns selected data with record context. These responses are unsigned.</dd>
        <dt>Building a register as well?</dt><dd><a href="/solutions/base-registry/">Base Registry Engine</a> stores and manages records for people, households, businesses, and other entities, with controlled changes and a history your team can inspect.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
</Product>
